<article class="env-lemma" id="09QG">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09QG">Lemma <span data-tag="09QG">12.3.10</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a preadditive category. Let $x \oplus y$ with morphisms $i, j, p, q$ as in Lemma <a href="/tag/0101" data-tag="0101">12.3.4</a> be a direct sum in $\mathcal{C}$. Then $i : x \to x \oplus y$ is a kernel of $q : x \oplus y \rightarrow y$. Dually, $p$ is a cokernel for $j$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $f : z' \to x \oplus y$ be a morphism such that $q \circ f = 0$. We have to show that there exists a unique morphism $g : z' \to x$ such that $f = i \circ g$. Since $i \circ p + j \circ q$ is the identity on $x \oplus y$ we see that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  f = (i \circ p + j \circ q) \circ f = i \circ p \circ f  \]
</div>
    <p>
       and hence $g = p \circ f$ works. Uniqueness holds because $p \circ i$ is the identity on $x$. The proof of the second statement is dual. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>